WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.930
So who's my guest this week?

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:05.260
It is the Democratic Senator,
Foreign Affairs Expert

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.668
and former vice presidential
candidate, Tim Kaine.

00:00:08.668 --> 00:00:10.870
I'm sending a clear message to the world.

00:00:10.870 --> 00:00:14.130
America is back.

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.280
America is back.

00:00:16.280 --> 00:00:18.870
But as we've seen with
the conflict in Ukraine,

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.343
diplomacy is far more
complicated than soundbites.

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.820
President Biden's performance
regarding Russia and Ukraine

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:28.440
hasn't been graded yet,

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.050
but for all the re-engagement

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.060
there have been missteps too.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:35.520
When that judgment is made,
my guest will play a part.

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:38.220
Sitting on the Senate
Committee on Foreign Relations,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.900
he's a Washington pro who's
been within spitting distance

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.260
of the vice presidency twice.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.377
Now he's focused on as he puts it,

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:48.570
"Protecting the Constitution
from the enemies of democracy,

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.620
foreign and domestic."

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.717
And after suffering from long COVID,

00:00:52.717 --> 00:00:55.620
"Ensuring those still
affected by the pandemic

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:56.627
are not forgotten."

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:00.950
With Russia accused of
war crimes in Ukraine.

00:01:00.950 --> 00:01:03.100
How does he think America and the world

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:05.390
should deal with Vladimir Putin?

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:07.160
And on a more personal note,

00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:10.890
how do you get over nearly
being vice president of America

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.890
twice?

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:13.503
When John Kerry became Secretary of State,

00:01:13.503 --> 00:01:16.680
he had a knock at the door that
morning and it's John McCain

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:17.660
He said, "I gotta tell you,

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.440
you and I are the only
people in the Senate

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.410
who have gone through what
you've just gone through.

00:01:22.410 --> 00:01:24.800
We are the only two now that
have been on a national ticket

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:27.320
and lost, and I'll tell you,
I've got the solution for you."

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:28.310
And I said, "I'm all ears."

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.700
He said, "Just go right back to work."

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:32.180
I said, "You found me here.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.100
First thing, first day we're open,

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.160
you found me here didn't you?"

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.160
There's been multiple
reports of war crimes,

00:01:37.160 --> 00:01:40.100
now, including the raping
of Ukrainian women.

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.770
Russian authorities have denied all.

00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:43.970
If the evidence is there,

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:48.120
do you think we'll ever see
Putin in The Hague on charges?

00:01:48.120 --> 00:01:50.390
When you target civilians
that's a war crime

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:51.740
and I believe he needs to get

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:53.680
the Slobodan Milošević treatment

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.523
and the US needs to press for that.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:10.810
Thank you so much for joining me.

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:12.510
Very good to have your company today,

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:13.720
Senator Kaine.
Indeed Emma.

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:18.110
I wanted to start talking about
how some have described it

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:21.760
as confusing, foreign policy
under President Biden.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.110
In one sense, we get told

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.250
at the beginning of his
administration, "America's back"

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:30.170
and we see quite quickly
in terms of diplomacy.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:34.080
A first major overseas act was
to withdraw from Afghanistan.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.790
And now we see engagement with Ukraine.

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:40.330
How would you define
how it is at the moment?

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:43.700
Emma I think the real key to

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.190
the Biden Administration, "America's back"

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.680
is America's back to
link arms with allies.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:50.880
That's the hallmark of this administration

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.420
that is the dramatic departure

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:55.860
from the Trump Administration.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:57.900
President Trump was a unilateral guy.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.700
He didn't like alliances,
he didn't like treaties.

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.410
He didn't like multiparty trade deals,

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:05.140
it was all one-on-one.

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:08.610
And that left many of
America's traditional allies

00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:09.680
wondering,

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:11.500
you know, is this just President Trump

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:14.640
or is the United States,
even the citizenry

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.810
kind of retreating from
linking arms with allies?

00:03:17.810 --> 00:03:21.430
Well, President Biden with a
long track record of chairing

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.150
the Foreign Relations
Committee in the Senate,

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:26.400
his work as Vice President
with leaders around the world

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.230
is back in a big way,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.130
whether it's getting back in
the Paris Climate Accords,

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:34.970
restarting negotiations with
Iran to see whether we can work

00:03:34.970 --> 00:03:38.870
in a multination deal to
stop a nuclear program there.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:43.830
But most recently the work
in Ukraine to assemble NATO

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.350
and non-NATO nations in a
very, very strong alliance

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:49.650
to put multiple pressure points on Russia

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.010
because of their illegal
invasion of Ukraine.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:55.030
I see this as an administration,
that's really committed

00:03:55.030 --> 00:03:56.980
to America's role in the world,

00:03:56.980 --> 00:03:59.350
but particularly in linking arms with,

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:01.640
with allies and growing
that number of allies.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:03.350
I suppose it's just, I'm very minded.

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:06.220
One of my guests, the Women's
Minister of Afghanistan.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:07.053
Yes.

00:04:07.053 --> 00:04:08.540
And she's now living out of a suitcase

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:10.890
as a refugee in London,

00:04:10.890 --> 00:04:14.580
thinking, "If America's back,
how am I in this situation?"

00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:17.360
And she described the most terrible escape

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:20.270
from Afghanistan under the Taliban.

00:04:20.270 --> 00:04:22.080
And that doesn't feel
like America is back.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:24.360
She's worried, she's
concerned that America

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:25.840
has forgotten Afghanistan.

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:28.700
Well, we haven't forgotten.
There are 76,000 Afghans

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:30.090
who are living in the United States,

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:31.950
who weren't living here in August.

00:04:31.950 --> 00:04:34.340
And Virginia, actually,
the State that I represent

00:04:34.340 --> 00:04:36.090
is one of the most common places.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:38.250
Folks came to Virginia initially,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.880
the resettlement processing
was largely done in Virginia.

00:04:41.880 --> 00:04:44.150
And the permanent
residences of many of these

00:04:44.150 --> 00:04:45.070
are in Virginia.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.450
Now there's many more who wanna come

00:04:46.450 --> 00:04:47.700
and we're gonna work on them,

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:49.760
but we have not forgotten that at all.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.970
But what President Biden concluded

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:54.870
and I agreed with this conclusion is,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.600
we had done as much with
the military as we could do.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:01.170
And the next chapter in
Afghan life had to be

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:02.970
primarily directed by Afghans

00:05:02.970 --> 00:05:06.500
and the continuing military presence

00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:09.930
of the US after 20 years,
should it be for 30 years?

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:11.030
should it be for 50 years?

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:13.680
And so he made that painful decision.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:14.740
And part of the decision,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:17.240
I think the only way to
understand it is also,

00:05:17.240 --> 00:05:19.760
we now view sort of the,

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:23.420
Russia and China as the great threats,

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:25.450
the great adversaries.

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.220
For a long time, we defined
our adversary primarily

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:30.200
as non-state terrorism,

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:32.730
but now we're seeing a different threat

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.530
from China and Russia in particular.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:38.410
And that means we have to
redeploy resources against the

00:05:38.410 --> 00:05:40.980
most significant challenges that we face.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:42.520
And that's another reason why

00:05:42.520 --> 00:05:45.510
20 years of military
investment in Afghanistan

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:46.343
should come to an end.

00:05:46.343 --> 00:05:47.790
If that makes sense on paper,

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:50.570
but the reality was very messy.

00:05:50.570 --> 00:05:52.620
It wasn't a good withdrawal.

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:54.530
And now the country's
on the brink of famine.

00:05:54.530 --> 00:05:58.560
And Yemen is, and Sudan
is, and Ethiopia is

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.020
and Ukraine as a bread basket of the world

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:03.110
that will create famine
issues around the country.

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:07.230
And we have an obligation
through our humanitarian budget

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:09.950
and in other diplomatic ways to try to

00:06:09.950 --> 00:06:13.400
provide support for
Afghans and we're going to.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.960
But the military engagement
needed to come to an end.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:20.980
Of course now looking back
and looking towards Ukraine

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:22.050
and what's happened there,

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:24.750
President Zelenskyy has given
an interview to the Economist

00:06:24.750 --> 00:06:27.130
in which he talked
about Western countries,

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:30.240
which either want a long
war to exhaust Russia,

00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:32.720
or those who are more
concerned about humanitarian

00:06:32.720 --> 00:06:35.820
and economic side effects
who wants a short war.

00:06:35.820 --> 00:06:38.008
Which camp does America fit into?

00:06:38.008 --> 00:06:39.560
We want a short war.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.818
The Biden Administration has decided that

00:06:41.818 --> 00:06:44.180
there should not be, at this moment,

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:47.860
there should not be US
Troops in or over Ukraine.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:51.030
But the scale of military
support that we're providing

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.797
Ukrainians is massive.

00:06:52.797 --> 00:06:55.720
$3 billion of US Military
aid went to Ukraine

00:06:55.720 --> 00:06:57.700
from 2014 through the end of '21

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.460
a billion of that in 2021.

00:07:00.460 --> 00:07:05.460
We've just voted to send about
$6.7 billion of military aid

00:07:05.480 --> 00:07:08.520
to Ukraine and an equal
amount of humanitarian aid

00:07:08.520 --> 00:07:10.760
and other nations are doing the same.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:14.586
But I will credit President Zelenskyy,

00:07:14.586 --> 00:07:19.560
we are not seeing the result
as quickly as any of us

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:20.393
would like.

00:07:20.393 --> 00:07:23.810
The economic pressure campaign,
canceling Nord Stream 2,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:27.870
UN resolutions, the intense sanctions,

00:07:27.870 --> 00:07:29.670
banning Russian oil imports.

00:07:29.670 --> 00:07:33.410
These are all having a very
significant effect on Russia,

00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:37.110
just as our military aid
is helping Ukrainians

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:38.860
extract great cost from Russia,

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:41.860
but we're not seeing a
positive outcome as quickly

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:42.730
as any of us would like.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.747
So do you think America's done enough?

00:07:44.747 --> 00:07:47.300
I think we can do more
and we are doing more.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:49.810
I think this very bipartisan vote

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:53.510
to do the $13.5 billion of
military and humanitarian aid

00:07:53.510 --> 00:07:54.780
combined with

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.880
the work that President
Biden and the team has done

00:07:56.880 --> 00:07:59.350
to encourage other countries to do a lot,

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:00.310
it's pretty significant.

00:08:00.310 --> 00:08:02.030
As you know, Emma, when you see

00:08:02.030 --> 00:08:04.840
non-NATO nations like Sweden and Finland,

00:08:04.840 --> 00:08:07.910
putting military aid into
the Ukrainian defense force

00:08:07.910 --> 00:08:09.850
and considering joining NATO,

00:08:09.850 --> 00:08:11.640
which would've been an
anathema five years ago

00:08:11.640 --> 00:08:12.473
in those countries.

00:08:12.473 --> 00:08:15.140
When you look at Germany
sending military aid to Ukraine,

00:08:15.140 --> 00:08:17.530
they've had a tradition of
not putting military aid

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:20.000
into conflict zones since
the end of World War II.

00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:22.540
When you look at Switzerland
being willing to play

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:24.770
a really important role in the sanctions

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:27.280
nations are getting off
the sidelines and realizing

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:29.880
this is a fight between
democracy and tyranny

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:31.300
and democracy has to win.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.330
There's been multiple
reports of war crimes.

00:08:33.330 --> 00:08:36.220
Now, including the raping
of Ukrainian women,

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:38.930
Russian authorities have denied all.

00:08:38.930 --> 00:08:40.140
If the evidence is there

00:08:40.140 --> 00:08:44.300
do you think we'll ever see
Putin in The Hague on charges?

00:08:44.300 --> 00:08:45.670
We need to.

00:08:45.670 --> 00:08:47.610
I started a call for this

00:08:47.610 --> 00:08:49.640
really at the end of the
first week of the war

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:50.720
at the end of February,

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:53.470
because it appeared to me that
there was this indiscriminate

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:57.050
targeting of civilians, which is,

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:59.240
you know, the war is
illegal in and of itself,

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:01.510
the invasion of sovereignty,
but when you target civilians,

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:02.650
that's a war crime.

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:04.140
And I believe he needs to get

00:09:04.140 --> 00:09:06.050
the Slobodan Milošević treatment

00:09:06.050 --> 00:09:08.250
and the US needs to press for that.

00:09:08.250 --> 00:09:10.663
Will we see him in The Hague?

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:14.660
We need to do everything we
can to make sure that he is.

00:09:14.660 --> 00:09:16.330
How could you do that?

00:09:16.330 --> 00:09:20.690
You would have to find some
agreement within the UN

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:21.730
to press forward on that

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.620
as was done in the case of the Balkans.

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:25.830
Now Russia, as a Security Council Member

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.790
has the ability within
the Security Council

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:29.200
to exercise a veto.

00:09:29.200 --> 00:09:30.520
But they don't have a veto on everything

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:31.750
within the General Assembly.

00:09:31.750 --> 00:09:35.000
So we would need to look
at the broader UN to see

00:09:35.000 --> 00:09:38.580
whether we could promote
a similar tribunal

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.640
as was the case in the Balkans.

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:43.520
Now, none of this will shame Putin.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:45.360
I don't believe he can be shamed,

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:50.010
but it might shame Russians
to be a pariah nation

00:09:50.010 --> 00:09:51.808
led by a leader who's a war criminal.

00:09:51.808 --> 00:09:54.060
So you see him as utterly shameless?

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:57.750
Personally, yes, but again, the,

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.890
not everyone in Russia,
not all the leadership

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:03.110
and certainly not the
citizenry are without shame.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.980
Russian people are strong
people and good people.

00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:08.720
And I think if they had
access to information,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.360
which they don't.

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:13.370
I think they would be ashamed

00:10:13.370 --> 00:10:15.260
that their nation is being led by somebody

00:10:15.260 --> 00:10:16.560
who is committing war crimes.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:19.180
How do you explain the invasion?

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:22.750
How can you try and understand
what it's all about?

00:10:22.750 --> 00:10:25.750
Because we're now looking at, as you say,

00:10:25.750 --> 00:10:28.970
a much longer fight
than perhaps either side

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:30.650
could ever have had imagined.

00:10:30.650 --> 00:10:31.790
I mean, it's an interesting question.

00:10:31.790 --> 00:10:33.880
Let me take you back a little bit.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:38.510
The US and European allies,
we're seeing the same data

00:10:38.510 --> 00:10:40.900
and President Zelenskyy too.

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:45.900
Amassing of troops on the
border, exercises in Belarus.

00:10:46.310 --> 00:10:48.490
But we were interpreting
the data differently.

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.170
And during my time in the Senate,

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.210
there hasn't been as wide a divergence.

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.620
The US was interpreting the data as

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:56.120
there's gonna be an invasion.

00:10:56.120 --> 00:10:58.730
Most of the European nations
and President Zelenskyy,

00:10:58.730 --> 00:11:00.140
they were at least saying,

00:11:00.140 --> 00:11:01.650
we don't think that's going to happen.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:03.940
And there might have been a little bit of

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:05.340
diplomacy in those words,

00:11:05.340 --> 00:11:07.780
but there was some sharp
difference of opinion

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:11.590
about whether this was just
a border exercise to flex,

00:11:11.590 --> 00:11:13.070
or whether there was gonna be an invasion.

00:11:13.070 --> 00:11:15.910
The US Intelligence was right about this.

00:11:15.910 --> 00:11:17.850
How do I explain it?

00:11:17.850 --> 00:11:21.147
I believe, although the
countries are very different

00:11:21.147 --> 00:11:22.930
and the individuals are very different.

00:11:22.930 --> 00:11:26.910
Vladimir Putin and Xi Jinping
are similar in that they

00:11:26.910 --> 00:11:29.990
each have an ideal for their
country that I would call,

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:33.570
they kind of view themselves
as historical rejuvenators.

00:11:33.570 --> 00:11:35.830
They're trying to
rejuvenate something that

00:11:35.830 --> 00:11:38.290
they felt was lost in the past.

00:11:38.290 --> 00:11:41.960
And that explains China
aspirations with respect to Taiwan.

00:11:41.960 --> 00:11:45.600
And it explains Putin's aspirations
with respect to Ukraine.

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:47.440
Particularly because in his view,

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:51.510
Modern Russia started with the
Christianization of Russia,

00:11:51.510 --> 00:11:53.660
which began in Ukraine.

00:11:53.660 --> 00:11:56.690
And so in his mind, there's
sort of this mythology

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:58.580
about what the greater Russia is

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:00.240
and Ukraine is very central to that.

00:12:00.240 --> 00:12:03.380
Can I ask you something about your leader,

00:12:03.380 --> 00:12:04.260
President Biden?

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:06.720
In March on a visit to Poland,

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.937
an important visit where
he said nine words,

00:12:09.937 --> 00:12:13.500
"For God's sake this man
cannot remain in power."

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:15.670
Talking about Putin.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:20.020
As someone who is very familiar
with how diplomacy works,

00:12:20.020 --> 00:12:21.640
how it should work.

00:12:21.640 --> 00:12:24.180
Where were you when you
heard what he had said?

00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:25.980
And what was your reaction?

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.060
Well, I wouldn't have said it.

00:12:28.060 --> 00:12:31.120
I wouldn't have said it because the US,

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:34.070
neither the United States,
nor the Biden Administration,

00:12:34.070 --> 00:12:37.150
considers regime changes as
an element of foreign policy.

00:12:37.150 --> 00:12:41.030
Now in the past, the US did
that and often got it wrong.

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:44.590
We failed so often when
we attempted to presume

00:12:44.590 --> 00:12:46.940
who another country's leader should be.

00:12:46.940 --> 00:12:48.977
President Biden said pretty plainly look,

00:12:48.977 --> 00:12:50.930
"Regime change isn't the US policy,

00:12:50.930 --> 00:12:52.910
but I was just speaking outta my sense of

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:57.070
moral outrage at the
activities of Vladimir Putin."

00:12:57.070 --> 00:12:58.030
And I credit him for that.

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:00.020
One of the things about Joe
Biden that I really like,

00:13:00.020 --> 00:13:02.020
is he's a man who speaks from the heart

00:13:02.020 --> 00:13:05.350
and it's not gonna
always be poll tested or

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:08.080
everything that has to be
written by five speech writers.

00:13:08.080 --> 00:13:10.210
And so he was expressing an outrage that

00:13:10.210 --> 00:13:12.110
frankly, the entire world feels,

00:13:12.110 --> 00:13:15.290
and those words did bring
some comfort to Ukrainians

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.500
and Russian opposition.

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:19.770
But the US doesn't have a
policy of regime change.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:22.930
But it was clumsy from
your ultimate diplomat,

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:24.110
more than clumsy.

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:25.160
In fact, I mean,

00:13:25.160 --> 00:13:28.010
I think it's important to hear
what you think about the idea

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:29.790
that this is the greatest gift

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:31.860
for the Russian propaganda machine.

00:13:31.860 --> 00:13:34.760
Well, I don't, again, I
wouldn't have said it,

00:13:34.760 --> 00:13:37.790
but I will say, one of Biden's strengths

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:39.940
is he plays from the heart.

00:13:39.940 --> 00:13:42.690
It's not all poll tested and it's not all

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:45.200
eight speech writers control what he says.

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:46.560
He plays from the heart and that--

00:13:46.560 --> 00:13:47.790
So did Donald Trump.

00:13:47.790 --> 00:13:52.610
That statement did express
a very sincere revulsion

00:13:52.610 --> 00:13:53.600
at what Putin is doing

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:55.470
the world should be revulsed.
If I put it

00:13:55.470 --> 00:13:56.740
I recognize you wouldn't say it,

00:13:56.740 --> 00:13:58.370
sorry to cut across you Senator but--

00:13:58.370 --> 00:13:59.203
Yeah.

00:13:59.203 --> 00:14:00.560
You know, if Donald Trump had said that

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:03.120
people on your side of politics

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:05.237
would be jumping all over that saying,

00:14:05.237 --> 00:14:07.540
"He doesn't know how to do diplomacy,

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:10.450
these are the sorts of gaffes
that we shouldn't be hearing,

00:14:10.450 --> 00:14:12.600
this isn't the way that we do politics."

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:15.300
Because his whole,
President Biden's whole play

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:17.700
was diplomacy is back.

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.130
The grown ups are back.
But I disagree with you.

00:14:20.130 --> 00:14:22.700
I don't think there's any
equivalence between the two.

00:14:22.700 --> 00:14:25.090
And frankly, what we had
with Donald Trump was

00:14:25.090 --> 00:14:27.030
cozying up to Vladimir Putin.

00:14:27.030 --> 00:14:29.240
I mean, at least Joe
Biden sees Vladimir Putin

00:14:29.240 --> 00:14:30.290
for who he is.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:31.260
I mean, let's be honest here.

00:14:31.260 --> 00:14:33.360
The guy has invaded a country illegally

00:14:33.360 --> 00:14:34.900
and he's committing war crimes.

00:14:34.900 --> 00:14:37.460
Joe Biden sees that for what it is,

00:14:37.460 --> 00:14:40.420
Donald Trump admired that
about Vladimir Putin.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:42.160
So there's no real
equivalence between 'em.

00:14:42.160 --> 00:14:45.300
Well, President Trump's not
in this particular scenario

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:46.960
at this time, of course, much criticism

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:49.560
has been leveled at him about
exactly as you described,

00:14:49.560 --> 00:14:51.710
but I'm more trying to say,

00:14:51.710 --> 00:14:54.690
here you've got the President
of the United States,

00:14:54.690 --> 00:14:57.650
billing himself as the
experienced diplomat.

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.460
And there he goes and says something,

00:14:59.460 --> 00:15:01.913
which really is a gift for Putin.

00:15:02.760 --> 00:15:05.330
All right, so put that
on one side of the ledger

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:07.670
and then on the other
side of the ledger put,

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:10.570
persuaded the Germans to cancel
the Nord Stream 2 pipeline,

00:15:10.570 --> 00:15:13.740
assembled an international
sanctions regime

00:15:13.740 --> 00:15:16.770
that's been joined by dozens
and dozens of countries.

00:15:16.770 --> 00:15:20.940
Reinvigorated NATO's commitment
to defensive democracies,

00:15:20.940 --> 00:15:24.510
got non-NATO nations to also
agree to send military aid

00:15:24.510 --> 00:15:25.620
into Ukraine.

00:15:25.620 --> 00:15:28.360
I agree you can put those comments

00:15:28.360 --> 00:15:30.240
on the negative side of the ledger,

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.250
but I think the positive
side of the ledger

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:35.480
is much longer with respect
to what's going on right now

00:15:35.480 --> 00:15:36.313
in Ukraine.

00:15:36.313 --> 00:15:38.037
A as you would see it, and
those are important points.

00:15:38.037 --> 00:15:40.670
And of course, many people
also talking about, you know,

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.410
nobody wanted this war,
nobody wanted this invasion,

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:47.280
but there has been greater
unity of NATO because of it.

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:49.470
And that strengthening of the alliance.

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:53.600
I suppose it just also
allows me to put to you

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:55.930
some of the concerns some have had

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:58.010
not just in America, but around the world

00:15:58.010 --> 00:16:00.180
that perhaps Joe Biden,

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:03.960
this maybe adds to the perception
that when he does ad-lib

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:06.320
and he gets it wrong
from the perspective of

00:16:06.320 --> 00:16:08.240
not recommending regime change,

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:11.730
that perhaps he isn't as fit
or quite as up to the job

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:13.170
as some have been concerned about.

00:16:13.170 --> 00:16:14.620
Polls in America have shown

00:16:14.620 --> 00:16:17.230
concerns about the fact he's nearly 80.

00:16:17.230 --> 00:16:19.240
And in November, for instance, his team

00:16:19.240 --> 00:16:21.730
put out a statement saying
he had developed a stiffer,

00:16:21.730 --> 00:16:25.010
less fluid gait as a result
of his advancing age.

00:16:25.010 --> 00:16:26.770
He's 80 years old,

00:16:26.770 --> 00:16:29.150
but I can tell you from
personal experience,

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:32.220
I was with the president two Fridays ago

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:34.840
when he invited me to the Oval Office

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:38.800
with the family of a New York
physician who died by suicide

00:16:38.800 --> 00:16:41.740
in the early days of
COVID in April of 2020.

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.410
And we got a bill passed in her honor

00:16:44.410 --> 00:16:47.330
to try to make sure that
our healthcare providers

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:49.350
get mental health
resources that they need.

00:16:49.350 --> 00:16:51.790
You know, I saw that in
the Oval Office, when he,

00:16:51.790 --> 00:16:54.910
you know, pulled aside
Lauren's mother and sister

00:16:54.910 --> 00:16:57.440
and brother-in-law side and
said, "Hey, I've lost two kids,

00:16:57.440 --> 00:16:58.887
I know how you feel."

00:16:58.887 --> 00:17:02.230
"And I admire that you're
taking the pain in your lives

00:17:02.230 --> 00:17:05.220
and you're trying to do something
good for others out of it

00:17:05.220 --> 00:17:06.680
and that's all we can do."

00:17:06.680 --> 00:17:08.273
In moments like that,

00:17:09.600 --> 00:17:12.120
you could hardly ask for a better leader

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:14.400
when you're connecting with
people in tough circumstances.

00:17:14.400 --> 00:17:16.760
And there's a lot of tough
things going on in our country

00:17:16.760 --> 00:17:17.965
and in the world right now and--

00:17:17.965 --> 00:17:18.798
There are.

00:17:18.798 --> 00:17:22.520
I think Joe Biden is sort
of a man for this moment.

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.050
Again and of course, you
know, describing that,

00:17:25.050 --> 00:17:27.210
I'm sure it was a very emotional time

00:17:27.210 --> 00:17:29.980
to be with those people and connecting

00:17:29.980 --> 00:17:32.270
through such emotional times
and such difficult times.

00:17:32.270 --> 00:17:34.970
And I know that you talking about COVID,

00:17:34.970 --> 00:17:38.480
have had your own experience
of connecting with people,

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:40.130
because I believe that

00:17:40.130 --> 00:17:42.250
you've been suffering from long COVID.

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:44.380
Yeah, I wouldn't use the word suffering,

00:17:44.380 --> 00:17:45.800
I would use the word experiencing,

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:49.710
I got COVID, Emma, right at
the beginning in March of 2020,

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.530
when we were here passing the CARES Act

00:17:51.530 --> 00:17:54.350
the first big COVID relief bill and

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:56.640
we still didn't understand
COVID that well,

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.650
and I had very non-standard symptoms,

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.300
but then as soon as I went
home, I gave COVID to my wife.

00:18:01.300 --> 00:18:02.920
Just one more thing to feel guilty about.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.557
And her symptoms were really normal,

00:18:04.557 --> 00:18:06.930
"So, oh, wow this is what we have."

00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:11.230
We had mild cases, both
of us, by mid April, 2020,

00:18:11.230 --> 00:18:13.430
we were essentially,
you know, back to work

00:18:13.430 --> 00:18:15.140
and doing everything we do.

00:18:15.140 --> 00:18:17.210
But I had this one weird thing,

00:18:17.210 --> 00:18:20.260
as soon as I got COVID just like this,

00:18:20.260 --> 00:18:23.430
every nerve ending in my
body just started to kinda

00:18:23.430 --> 00:18:26.890
tingle or buzz 24/7, 365.

00:18:26.890 --> 00:18:28.287
I describe it as like,

00:18:28.287 --> 00:18:30.730
"Every nerve ending has
had five cups of coffee."

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:33.660
And it's not painful and I
can work and I can exercise

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:34.740
and I can sleep.

00:18:34.740 --> 00:18:37.120
I recognize that you don't
wanna say you are suffering

00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:39.820
in light of those who are having
far more serious symptoms.

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:41.730
But that is still a striking thing

00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:44.670
that your body doesn't feel
the same as it used to.

00:18:44.670 --> 00:18:45.503
Yes.
And,

00:18:45.503 --> 00:18:47.980
you know, it must be
frustrating, I imagine at times,

00:18:47.980 --> 00:18:50.700
just to feel different in yourself.

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:54.600
Yeah it is, it's just, it's
frustrating I will say that.

00:18:54.600 --> 00:18:56.770
I, you know, a neurologist told me,

00:18:56.770 --> 00:18:59.810
I mean, for folks who
might be experiencing this.

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:03.580
That a neurological after-effect
of a virus is not uncommon.

00:19:03.580 --> 00:19:05.080
It's not the most common thing,

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:06.470
but it's also not uncommon.

00:19:06.470 --> 00:19:08.530
And not just COVID other viruses

00:19:08.530 --> 00:19:10.180
can have a neurological after-effect.

00:19:10.180 --> 00:19:12.990
And my neurologist said, "You
know, look, the good news is

00:19:12.990 --> 00:19:15.840
the neurological after effects
usually don't get worse.

00:19:15.840 --> 00:19:18.360
The bad news is they may not get better."

00:19:18.360 --> 00:19:21.910
And it has just been sort of
constant now for two years.

00:19:21.910 --> 00:19:24.750
It's not even a new normal because I just,

00:19:24.750 --> 00:19:26.480
if it was a new normal,
I wouldn't notice it,

00:19:26.480 --> 00:19:28.800
but I notice it all the time.

00:19:28.800 --> 00:19:30.880
But again, it just connects me

00:19:30.880 --> 00:19:33.040
with people who were dealing with it and

00:19:33.040 --> 00:19:35.550
I started to talk about
it in committee hearings,

00:19:35.550 --> 00:19:36.397
because I wanted folks watching to know,

00:19:36.397 --> 00:19:38.240
there was at least somebody

00:19:38.240 --> 00:19:40.270
on the dais who believed them, many

00:19:40.270 --> 00:19:42.310
long COVID sufferers
are not being believed.

00:19:42.310 --> 00:19:44.230
Yes, but just 'cause
you mentioned work there

00:19:44.230 --> 00:19:47.770
and also COVID and the time
of reflection people have had,

00:19:47.770 --> 00:19:49.290
I was looking back at your career

00:19:49.290 --> 00:19:50.530
and thinking about what you

00:19:50.530 --> 00:19:52.590
have or haven't done or nearly done.

00:19:52.590 --> 00:19:55.750
And you've nearly been
vice president twice

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:56.760
I understand.

00:19:56.760 --> 00:19:58.650
Nearly to President Obama,

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:00.260
losing out to a certain Joe Biden

00:20:00.260 --> 00:20:02.870
and then nearly of course,
with Hillary Clinton,

00:20:02.870 --> 00:20:04.630
winning the popular vote.

00:20:04.630 --> 00:20:06.820
But still just not getting there.

00:20:06.820 --> 00:20:09.200
How have you come to
peace with that Senator?

00:20:09.200 --> 00:20:11.933
I call myself an electoral
college drop out.

00:20:13.440 --> 00:20:14.273
You know,

00:20:15.310 --> 00:20:17.350
if you're in this line of work,

00:20:17.350 --> 00:20:18.950
there's wins and losses.

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:20.980
Before I was in politics,

00:20:20.980 --> 00:20:24.970
I had a very high stakes
legal practice trying cases.

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:26.990
I won the biggest civil
rights jury verdict

00:20:26.990 --> 00:20:28.670
in the history of the United States.

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:31.400
I also walked somebody into
the death chamber in Virginia

00:20:31.400 --> 00:20:33.210
and held his hand while
they strapped them down

00:20:33.210 --> 00:20:34.440
to execute him.

00:20:34.440 --> 00:20:36.120
You have wins and losses.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:39.450
And you know, life just produces that,

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:42.450
as soon as you think things
are going well, watch out

00:20:42.450 --> 00:20:44.260
and on your darkest day,

00:20:44.260 --> 00:20:45.530
a window will open.

00:20:45.530 --> 00:20:47.820
And that's, you know, I'm 64,

00:20:47.820 --> 00:20:50.770
I'm a lot wiser than I was
when I was half my age.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:53.740
But I just feel like a life fully lived

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:55.530
is going to be ups and downs.

00:20:55.530 --> 00:20:56.620
And you know, you're right,

00:20:56.620 --> 00:20:58.140
I was nearly vice president twice,

00:20:58.140 --> 00:20:59.060
and what that tells me is,

00:20:59.060 --> 00:21:00.320
I'm not supposed to be vice president

00:21:00.320 --> 00:21:01.707
and so,

00:21:01.707 --> 00:21:03.420
and I love being a Senator.

00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:04.560
I love being a Senator.

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:08.850
It was tough to be on a
ticket in 2016 and lose.

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.100
I was so thrilled.

00:21:10.100 --> 00:21:12.190
I helped President Obama become

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:14.600
the first African American
President of the United States.

00:21:14.600 --> 00:21:17.140
I threw everything into
helping Hillary Clinton

00:21:17.140 --> 00:21:19.410
become the first woman
President of the United States.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:22.320
To win the popular vote
by a significant margin

00:21:22.320 --> 00:21:24.240
and yet fall short.

00:21:24.240 --> 00:21:27.290
Yeah, it was tough, but
I just came back to work.

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:29.530
The next day the Senate was open and

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:31.396
just started working hard and
that's what I've been doing

00:21:31.396 --> 00:21:32.450
since 2016.
Really?

00:21:32.450 --> 00:21:34.707
You just went back in the next time.

00:21:34.707 --> 00:21:36.270
I'll tell you an interesting story.

00:21:36.270 --> 00:21:39.500
We conceded in a speech Wednesday,

00:21:39.500 --> 00:21:42.230
the day after the election, the
Senate was closed that week.

00:21:42.230 --> 00:21:44.430
The Senate reopened on Monday.

00:21:44.430 --> 00:21:46.330
Came back to work early Monday morning.

00:21:46.330 --> 00:21:50.150
First colleague who came him
to see me on that first Monday

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:53.250
after the election in
2016 was John McCain.

00:21:53.250 --> 00:21:56.680
I'm in the office that
John McCain used to have.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.330
He moved around the corner
to take John Kerry's office

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:01.237
when John Kerry became Secretary of State.

00:22:01.237 --> 00:22:04.150
Get a knock at the door that
morning and it's John McCain.

00:22:04.150 --> 00:22:05.807
He said, "I want to come
see you, I gotta tell you."

00:22:05.807 --> 00:22:08.670
"You and I are the only
people in the Senate

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:10.610
who have gone through what
you've just gone through.

00:22:10.610 --> 00:22:13.030
We are the only two now that
have been on a national ticket

00:22:13.030 --> 00:22:14.090
and lost.

00:22:14.090 --> 00:22:15.590
And I was running for president,

00:22:15.590 --> 00:22:17.640
so it's even more painful than
what you're going through.

00:22:17.640 --> 00:22:19.710
And I'll tell you, I've
got the solution for you."

00:22:19.710 --> 00:22:20.730
And I said, "I'm all ears."

00:22:20.730 --> 00:22:23.090
He said, "Just go right back to work."

00:22:23.090 --> 00:22:24.570
I said, "You found me here.

00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:26.510
First thing, first day we're open,

00:22:26.510 --> 00:22:27.510
you found me here, didn't you?"

00:22:27.510 --> 00:22:29.100
And then John used to say to me,

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:31.080
he was my chairman in Armed Services.

00:22:31.080 --> 00:22:32.877
About every three or
four months, he'd say,

00:22:32.877 --> 00:22:34.320
"Are you doing what I told you?

00:22:34.320 --> 00:22:35.310
Are you doing what I told you?"

00:22:35.310 --> 00:22:39.310
And so he kind of, you know,
was giving me a pep talk.

00:22:39.310 --> 00:22:43.080
But that really is the
answer 'cause every day,

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:44.650
every day my staff and I,

00:22:44.650 --> 00:22:47.130
we have the opportunity to
lighten somebody's burden,

00:22:47.130 --> 00:22:50.320
help a veterans, get a, get,
you know, of veterans benefit.

00:22:50.320 --> 00:22:53.540
Help stop somebody from
getting their home foreclosed.

00:22:53.540 --> 00:22:55.810
I got a great bill passed in
the Foreign Relations Committee

00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:57.690
yesterday, sort of a historic one.

00:22:57.690 --> 00:23:00.760
If we can get it finally
done to say that no president

00:23:00.760 --> 00:23:03.560
of the United States
can withdraw from NATO

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.030
without congressional approval.

00:23:05.030 --> 00:23:07.870
So every day we've got a
chance to do something good,

00:23:07.870 --> 00:23:09.530
and most days we do.

00:23:09.530 --> 00:23:13.213
And you know that helps you
get over the sting of a loss.

00:23:13.213 --> 00:23:14.697
I mean, I thought he was gonna say,

00:23:14.697 --> 00:23:16.590
"I've got the answer
for you, here's a beer

00:23:16.590 --> 00:23:17.873
and here's a whiskey chaser."

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:19.863
I mean...

00:23:19.863 --> 00:23:22.510
Well, we did that too,
but that was not the,

00:23:22.510 --> 00:23:24.920
that would've been win or lose.

00:23:24.920 --> 00:23:28.250
He was, this was the
advice for, if you've lost.

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:29.990
It's been fascinating
to talk to you, Senator.

00:23:29.990 --> 00:23:31.790
Thank you very much for your time.

00:23:31.790 --> 00:23:32.675
I'm really glad we could do this.

00:23:32.675 --> 00:23:33.508
Thank you.

00:23:33.508 --> 00:23:36.020
And thank you for being
with us so much then.

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:38.713
Until we meet again,
take care and goodbye.

